WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.327
Last time ended with a target on level

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:05.437
ground, seventy-two metres from a launcher that fires

00:00:05.517 --> 00:00:07.082
at thirty metres per second.

00:00:07.765 --> 00:00:08.960
Two paths reach it.

00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:12.035
One stays low, peaking at under nine metres.

00:00:12.477 --> 00:00:15.011
The other climbs to about thirty-seven metres before

00:00:15.053 --> 00:00:15.635
it comes down.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.189
If both are launched at the same instant,

00:00:18.672 --> 00:00:20.138
which reaches the target first?

00:00:20.985 --> 00:00:22.171
Or do they arrive together?

00:00:26.227 --> 00:00:27.159
Let's launch them together.

00:00:27.682 --> 00:00:29.813
The low one arrives after about two point

00:00:29.893 --> 00:00:30.998
six seven seconds.

00:00:31.562 --> 00:00:32.957
The high one is still up there.

00:00:33.563 --> 00:00:36.352
It finally lands after about five point five

00:00:36.473 --> 00:00:38.820
one seconds, in exactly the same spot.

00:00:39.543 --> 00:00:42.670
Same speed, same target, two angles, two very

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:43.472
different flights.

00:00:44.336 --> 00:00:46.321
So why are there two angles, and how

00:00:46.342 --> 00:00:47.103
do we find them both?

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.267
For level ground we already have a shortcut,

00:00:50.708 --> 00:00:51.571
the range equation.

00:00:52.435 --> 00:00:54.911
R equals u squared sin two theta over

00:00:54.952 --> 00:00:55.193
g.

00:00:55.776 --> 00:00:58.321
Remember its conditions: launch and landing at the

00:00:58.361 --> 00:01:01.227
same height, no air resistance, and constant g.

00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:03.104
It can run backwards, too.

00:01:03.831 --> 00:01:06.602
Say a projectile launched at thirty-eight degrees lands

00:01:06.803 --> 00:01:08.791
forty-five metres away on level ground.

00:01:09.494 --> 00:01:10.416
Rearrange for u:

00:01:11.138 --> 00:01:13.002
u is the square root of R g

00:01:13.123 --> 00:01:14.145
over sin two theta.

00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:16.329
That's the square root of four hundred and

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.378
forty-one over sin seventy-six degrees, about twenty-one point

00:01:20.459 --> 00:01:21.560
three metres per second.

00:01:22.163 --> 00:01:23.038
Now our target.

00:01:23.824 --> 00:01:25.895
This time we know u and R, and

00:01:25.935 --> 00:01:26.780
we want theta.

00:01:27.583 --> 00:01:30.912
Rearranging, sin two theta equals R g over

00:01:31.093 --> 00:01:31.534
u squared.

00:01:32.116 --> 00:01:35.146
That's seventy-two times nine point eight over nine

00:01:35.226 --> 00:01:37.835
hundred, which is zero point seven eight four.

00:01:38.678 --> 00:01:41.866
Your calculator's inverse sine gives two theta as

00:01:41.987 --> 00:01:43.591
fifty-one point six degrees,

00:01:44.193 --> 00:01:46.920
so theta is twenty-five point eight degrees.

00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:48.410
That's the low path.

00:01:49.218 --> 00:01:51.213
But the calculator only gave us one answer.

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:52.207
Where's the other?

00:01:53.235 --> 00:01:54.959
Look at the sine graph from zero to

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.142
one hundred and eighty degrees.

00:01:56.764 --> 00:01:59.649
It rises to one at ninety degrees, then

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.353
falls back again, symmetrically.

00:02:02.115 --> 00:02:04.156
So a height of zero point seven eight

00:02:04.278 --> 00:02:05.738
four is reached twice:

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.981
at fifty-one point six degrees,

00:02:08.602 --> 00:02:10.483
and at one hundred and eighty minus that,

00:02:10.845 --> 00:02:12.305
one hundred and twenty-eight point four.

00:02:13.027 --> 00:02:15.552
Halve it, and theta is sixty-four point two

00:02:15.573 --> 00:02:15.933
degrees,

00:02:16.455 --> 00:02:17.196
the high path.

00:02:18.058 --> 00:02:20.583
Notice that twenty-five point eight plus sixty-four point

00:02:20.644 --> 00:02:21.866
two is ninety.

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:24.614
Angles that add to ninety degrees are called

00:02:24.775 --> 00:02:25.436
complementary.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.964
One warning: subtract from one eighty before you

00:02:29.005 --> 00:02:30.207
halve, not after.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.195
One eighty minus twenty-five point eight would aim

00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:34.158
the launcher backwards.

00:02:35.023 --> 00:02:35.628
That's the maths.

00:02:36.112 --> 00:02:36.777
Here's the physics.

00:02:37.544 --> 00:02:38.718
Resolve both launches.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:41.066
The low one has u x of about

00:02:41.146 --> 00:02:43.972
twenty-seven metres per second, and u y of

00:02:44.012 --> 00:02:45.275
about thirteen point one.

00:02:45.997 --> 00:02:47.962
The high one has exactly the same two

00:02:48.023 --> 00:02:48.344
numbers,

00:02:48.885 --> 00:02:49.246
swapped.

00:02:50.028 --> 00:02:51.693
Now think about what range is:

00:02:52.214 --> 00:02:54.500
horizontal velocity times the time in the air.

00:02:55.243 --> 00:02:56.948
The time in the air depends only on

00:02:56.969 --> 00:02:57.169
u y.

00:02:58.374 --> 00:03:00.501
So the low launch is fast across but

00:03:00.582 --> 00:03:02.870
brief, and the high launch is slow across

00:03:03.031 --> 00:03:04.074
but stays up longer.

00:03:04.818 --> 00:03:07.468
Swapping the components swaps those two effects, and

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:09.697
their product, the range, doesn't change.

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:11.315
Let's put numbers on that.

00:03:11.859 --> 00:03:13.971
On level ground, the flight time is two

00:03:14.153 --> 00:03:15.097
u y over g.

00:03:15.741 --> 00:03:18.105
For the low path, that's two times thirteen

00:03:18.146 --> 00:03:20.689
point one over nine point eight, about two

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.871
point six seven seconds,

00:03:22.453 --> 00:03:24.917
and twenty-seven times that is seventy-two metres.

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:28.443
For the high path, it's two times twenty-seven

00:03:28.605 --> 00:03:29.525
over nine point eight,

00:03:30.027 --> 00:03:31.870
about five point five one seconds,

00:03:32.552 --> 00:03:34.815
and thirteen point one times that is also

00:03:34.916 --> 00:03:35.737
seventy-two metres.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:37.964
Two flights, different times,

00:03:38.547 --> 00:03:39.268
one landing point.

00:03:40.176 --> 00:03:41.642
Is there always a second angle?

00:03:42.326 --> 00:03:44.589
Push the target further away, and the two

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.252
angles move towards each other,

00:03:46.814 --> 00:03:48.537
until they meet at forty-five degrees.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.862
There, u x equals u

00:03:51.405 --> 00:03:53.229
y, and their product is as big as

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:53.730
it can be.

00:03:54.493 --> 00:03:56.736
At thirty metres per second that range is

00:03:56.917 --> 00:03:59.942
u squared over g, about ninety-one point eight

00:03:59.983 --> 00:04:02.206
metres, and only one angle works.

00:04:02.929 --> 00:04:03.450
Beyond that,

00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:06.099
R g over u squared is bigger than

00:04:06.261 --> 00:04:08.428
one, and no angle has a sine bigger

00:04:08.469 --> 00:04:08.789
than one.

00:04:09.412 --> 00:04:10.858
Your calculator shows an error,

00:04:11.421 --> 00:04:13.148
and that's the physics telling you the target

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:13.932
is out of reach.

00:04:14.952 --> 00:04:15.861
Here's a way to remember it.

00:04:16.468 --> 00:04:18.794
Complementary angles swap the components,

00:04:19.396 --> 00:04:21.421
and the range depends only on their product.

00:04:21.983 --> 00:04:22.706
Swap the parts,

00:04:23.209 --> 00:04:23.872
keep the product.

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:26.224
But this only works on level ground.

00:04:26.988 --> 00:04:28.652
Launch from a cliff, and the two angles

00:04:28.693 --> 00:04:31.019
that hit a target are no longer complementary.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:34.333
Your turn.

00:04:35.118 --> 00:04:38.001
A projectile is launched at fourteen point zero

00:04:38.042 --> 00:04:40.004
metres per second over level ground.

00:04:40.626 --> 00:04:41.949
Neglecting air resistance,

00:04:42.471 --> 00:04:45.137
find both launch angles that give a horizontal

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:47.163
range of ten point zero metres.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:48.861
Pause here to answer.

00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:02.609
sin two theta equals ten times nine point

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:06.231
eight over fourteen squared, which is exactly zero

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:06.832
point five.

00:05:07.614 --> 00:05:10.015
So two theta is thirty degrees or one

00:05:10.036 --> 00:05:10.935
hundred and fifty degrees,

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:15.019
and theta is fifteen degrees or seventy-five degrees.

00:05:15.622 --> 00:05:17.234
They add to ninety, as they should.

00:05:18.182 --> 00:05:20.724
The classic slip is stopping at fifteen degrees,

00:05:21.005 --> 00:05:22.706
the only angle the calculator gives.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:24.635
Last one.

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:28.071
A target is twenty point zero metres away,

00:05:28.653 --> 00:05:30.295
at the same height as the launch point.

00:05:31.017 --> 00:05:34.064
Neglecting air resistance, find the minimum launch speed

00:05:34.125 --> 00:05:34.826
that can reach it,

00:05:35.369 --> 00:05:36.751
and the launch angle needed.

00:05:37.535 --> 00:05:38.302
Pause here to answer.

00:05:48.988 --> 00:05:51.659
The range is biggest when sin two theta

00:05:51.921 --> 00:05:53.828
is one, at forty-five degrees.

00:05:54.471 --> 00:05:56.092
So the minimum speed satisfies R

00:05:56.614 --> 00:05:59.397
equals u squared over g, and u is

00:05:59.438 --> 00:06:01.580
the square root of twenty times nine point

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:03.563
eight, the square root of one hundred and

00:06:03.644 --> 00:06:07.368
ninety-six, exactly fourteen point zero metres per second.

00:06:08.173 --> 00:06:09.620
Any slower, and the sine would have to

00:06:09.661 --> 00:06:10.324
be bigger than one.

00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:13.316
The slip is to aim at ninety degrees

00:06:13.598 --> 00:06:15.102
because it makes sin theta biggest.

00:06:15.584 --> 00:06:17.132
Straight up has no range at all.

00:06:17.636 --> 00:06:19.344
It's sin two theta that matters.

00:06:21.309 --> 00:06:23.195
Every prediction so far has come from a

00:06:23.357 --> 00:06:26.107
model, with no air resistance and a constant

00:06:26.148 --> 00:06:26.388
g.

00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:28.343
But how do we know the model is

00:06:28.404 --> 00:06:28.806
any good?

00:06:29.596 --> 00:06:30.124
We test it.

00:06:31.012 --> 00:06:32.792
Next time, a ball rolls down a ramp,

00:06:33.054 --> 00:06:35.094
flies off it horizontally and lands on the

00:06:35.115 --> 00:06:35.354
bench,

00:06:35.856 --> 00:06:37.876
and we'll see how our own measurements can

00:06:37.917 --> 00:06:39.137
put the model to the test.

00:06:41.928 --> 00:06:42.750
Here are the key notes.

00:06:43.274 --> 00:06:44.821
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:44.862 --> 00:06:45.283
to copy them.
